WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.560
Last thing to note here,

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.950
and this is really true
against any exercise program,

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.340
for any goal, is we have to understand

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.030
how we're modifying and really
be intentional and judicious

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.090
about all seven of the
modifiable variables.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.950
If you've not heard that phrase before,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.260
I would recommend checking
out my guest series

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.110
with Dr. Andrew Huberman
on the Huberman Lab podcast

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.730
I did in 2023, and we
talked about them in detail.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.140
So to go through them quickly here,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.560
I use an acronym developed
by my former colleague

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.520
and lab mate, Dr. Lee Brown,
which is called COFIVRP.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.600
It's a wonderful acronym.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.240
Scientists are always great
at developing acronyms.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.880
But it stands for choice,
which is exercise choice,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.070
so what exercises are you selecting?

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.070
This includes equipment as
well as what movement patterns,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.380
and a lot of the things I
just discussed a second ago.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.680
So the choice of the
exercise you're selecting

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.980
influences in part what
adaptation you're going to get:

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.260
strength and power or hypertrophy.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.120
O, which stands for order,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.860
it's another way of what
order do I do them in.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.230
So what exercise goes first
in my day, second, third,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.300
fourth, fifth, et cetera.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.580
In the case of things
like strength and power,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.650
order is incredibly important.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.720
You need to be hyperspecific.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.273
With hypertrophy, it's far less important.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.860
The next one is V for volume.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.390
This is how many repetitions

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.670
multiplied by how many
sets throughout the week.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.180
So what's my total
weekly or monthly volume?

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.890
Again, as I said a second ago,

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.510
it's important in both categories here,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:31.650
that we're paying attention,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:32.820
but really, we need to make sure

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.240
that we're not losing volume
over time with hypertrophy.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.890
And for strength we just want to make sure

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.420
we're not doing so much volume

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.730
that it's compromising
our ability to come back

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.980
and use high force and high power

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.030
in our next training session,

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.790
because those are the
ultimate drivers, generally,

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.740
for those adaptations.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.290
The next is I for intensity,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.550
and that refers to, again,

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.950
what percentage of your
one repetition maximum

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.940
you're going after.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.490
It's the inverse answer to
what I just said a second ago,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.750
where intensity needs to be
key for power and strength

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.220
and where it is important,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.760
but far less relevant for
actual muscle hypertrophy.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.390
F stands for frequency. This
is how many days per week.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.770
Easy distinction here.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.990
When you're training for hypertrophy,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.450
you don't necessarily want
to train the same muscle

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.680
on multiple days in a row,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.990
because you need to allow some recovery.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.510
Now, where a lot of people
actually make the mistake here,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.960
you don't need the muscles
to have complete rest.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.520
It doesn't need to be
completely turned off

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.690
for two or three days.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.570
As you'll see in the
program, it's not needed,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.070
but you definitely don't
want to work it hard

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.880
multiple days in a row
in your typical fashion.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.350
Strength and power is the opposite.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.360
You can train the same
muscles every single day.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.070
As you'll see, the program does.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:44.940
Not a problem here,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.130
because there's not a
lot of residual fatigue,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.840
not a lot of local fatigue,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.370
rather like in the actual muscle,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.270
not a lot of soreness

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.400
because we're not pushing that adaptation.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.380
And so frequency can be very different

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.260
between these two programs and should be.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.120
Strength and power requires practice,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.170
which means more repetitions,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.210
which means a lot more frequently.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.820
R stands for rest, which
is your rest interval.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.810
How much time am I taking
between sets or even repetitions?

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.420
You have the option: for
hypertrophy, can be low rest,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.600
say 30 seconds of rest,
or maybe even less,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.030
all the way up to five minutes of rest;

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.160
but for strength and power, almost always,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.590
you need to have a minimum
of three minutes of rest,

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.780
because again we're going
after something different here.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.790
This is really about the quality

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:27.840
of each individual movement,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.080
and so if we're keeping
ourselves to a short rest

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.360
and we're losing our
force or power production,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.250
then we're losing our actual goal,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.710
which means we're not going
to get what we're after.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.120
There are exceptions to all these rules,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.860
but this is just a standard default

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.290
of typically what we're trying to do.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.930
The last variable here
stands for P, progression,

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.500
which is how are we progressing
these things over time?

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.090
And that is really what
we're going to talk about

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:51.923
along the way.

00:03:51.923 --> 00:03:53.923
[music]

